 ],
 "literal": "The excellent-headed palmyra palm's tender, great stem-horse, / harnessing it according to custom with a big garland adorned with bells, / wearing white bones, appearing so that others mock, / casting aside great modesty in the span of a single day, / will it make her walk in the street? / The naive girl of gentle, swaying gait / did not relent to the messengers we sent.",
 "translation": "The naive girl of gentle, swaying gait did not relent to the messengers we sent. Will riding the palmyra horse—made of the tender, large stems of the high-topped palmyra palm, harnessed according to custom with a large garland of bells, wearing white bones, appearing in public to be mocked by others—make her cast aside her great modesty in a single day and come out into the street?",
 "modern": "மென்மையான அசைந்து செல்லும் நடையையுடைய பேதைப்பெண் நாம் அனுப்பிய தூதுகளுக்கு இரங்கவில்லை. சிறந்த உச்சியையுடைய பனைமரத்தின் இளமையான பெரிய மடலால் செய்த குதிரையில், முறைப்படி மணிகள் கட்டிய பெரிய மாலையைப் பூட்டி, வெண்மையான எலும்புகளை அணிந்து கொண்டு, பிறர் பார்த்துப் பரிகசிக்கும்படி தோன்றி, ஒருநாளுக்குள் அவளுடைய பெரிய நாணத்தைத் தவிர்த்து, அவளைத் தெருவில் வரச்செய்யுமா இந்த மடல் குதிரை?",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the hero, expressing his despair to himself or his companion in the Kurinji landscape. He is deeply in love with the heroine, but she has not responded to his love, and the messengers he sent to her have failed to move her heart. In desperation, he contemplates the ancient custom of 'maṭalēṟal', where a lover rides a painful horse made of sharp palmyra stems to publicize his unrequited love. He wonders if this extreme public act—wearing bones, ringing bells, and exposing himself to ridicule—will finally force his gentle, modest beloved to cast aside her shame and come out into the street to accept him. The contrast between the rough, painful palmyra stems and the girl's gentle, swaying gait highlights the intensity of his suffering.",
